WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:00.970
In 2007,

2
00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.970
Dr. Mackill and the International Rice
Research Institute led a field

3
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.340
experiment with a strain of rice, IR64,

4
00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:15.080
that contained the Sub1 gene. On day 25,
the fields flooded for around 17 days.

5
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.070
As the water began to clear, we see
that many of the regular IR64 plants

6
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:25.290
have died, and those that survived have
severely stunted growth. In contrast,

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.930
all of the IR64 plants containing Sub1
survived the flood. The whole plot is

8
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:35.580
full of healthy rice plants, while the
regular IR64 side has only sparse

9
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.920
patches of growth. The final harvested
rice piles show us a wonderful result.

10
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.370
The yield of IR64+Sub1

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.150
rice is double that of plain IR64.

12
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.180
Sub1 has saved the day.

13
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.860
With that successful test, Sub1
varieties of rice exploded in

14
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.220
popularity throughout Asia. Farmers
across the region began adopting these

15
00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.150
submergence tolerant rice strains, and
as floods came through, their rice

16
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:06.070
continued to thrive. In addition, they
claimed, the Sub1 rice tasted just as

17
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.810
good as their traditional rice strains,
further boosting their adoption by

18
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:14.960
other farmers. Let's listen to the
story of Maha Jha, an actual farmer in

19
00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.420
East India who planted Sub1 rice.

20
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.330
Currently, there are over 5 million
farmers in Asia growing Sub1 varieties

21
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.360
of rice, which has brought food
security to over 70 million people

22
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.380
across the continent.

